==English==

===Etymology===
Likely from dialectal {{m|en|dodge}}, {{m|en|dod}}, {{m|en|dodd|t=to jog, trudge along, totter", also "to jerk, jig}}, of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin. Perhaps from unrecorded {{inh|en|enm|*dodden}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gem-pro|*dud-|t=to move}}, related to {{cog|ang|dydrian}}, {{m|ang|dyderian|t=to delude, deceive}}, {{cog|enm|dideren|t=to tremble, quake, shiver}}, {{cog|en|dodder}}, {{cog|no|dudra|t=to tremble}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɒd͡ʒ/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɑd͡ʒ/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-dodge.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒdʒ|s=1}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[avoid]] (something) by [[moving]] [[suddenly]] out of the [[way]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''dodged''' traffic crossing the street.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=The Battle of Samar (Alternate History) - Bring on the Battleships!|date=6 March 2019|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJJWG0viaZQ|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220720021028/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EJJWG0viaZQ|archivedate=20 July 2022|accessdate=22 July 2022|time=26:48|text=''But'' that was only the start, because the ''Fletcher''s - (obviously) carrying ''two'' torpedo launchers - were only launching half-salvos, so one full wave of torpedoes had driven off the cruisers after having savaged the destroyers, aaand then it was a case of, well, here come twenty-five destroyers, here comes{{sic}} two hundred and fifty torpedoes, hello Japanese battleships, '''dodge''' ''this!''}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To [[avoid]]; to [[sidestep]].
#: {{ux|en|The politician '''dodged''' the question with a meaningless reply.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|}} To [[elude]].
# {{lb|en|archaic|ambitransitive}} To go, or cause to go, [[hither and thither]].
#* {{quote-book|en
|year=1862
|author=w:George Borrow
|title=w:Wild Wales
|passage=Or if a footpad asks him for his money, what need he care provided he has an umbrella? He threatens to '''dodge''' the ferrule into the ruffian’s eye, and the fellow starts back and says, “Lord, sir! I meant no harm. {{...}}
}}
# {{lb|en|photography|videography}} To make an area of an image lighter {{q|when processing [[photograph]]s in a [[darkroom]], this is accomplished by decreasing the [[exposure]] of that area to light}}.
#: {{cot|en|burn}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To follow by dodging, or suddenly shifting from place to place.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1782|author=w:Frances Burney|title=Cecilia|section=II.iii.7
|passage="I had a notion he was '''dodging''' me all the way I came, for I saw him just behind me, turn which way I would."}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Coleridge Lyrical Ballads|poem=The Rime of the Ancyent Marinere|passage=A speck, a mist, a shape, I wist! / And still it neared and neared: / As if it '''dodged''' a water-sprite, / It plunged and tacked and veered.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Haunted House|page=30|story=The Ghost in Master B's Room|passage=Miss Griffin screamed after me, the faithless Vizier ran after me, and the boy at the turnpike '''dodged''' me into a corner, like a sheep, and cut me off.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|dated}} To [[trick]] somebody.

