# {{lb|en|law|historical}} [[land|Land]] [[held]] of a [[superior#Noun|superior]], [[particularly]] [[on condition]] of [[homage]], [[fealty]], and [[personal]] [[service]], [[especially]] [[military service]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Randle Cotgrave|chapter=Fief|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ISi4M196tl4C&pg=PT291|title=A French and English Dictionary|location=London|publisher=Anthony Dolle|year=1673|oclc=83780859|passage=Fief: m[asculine] ''A'' '''Fief'''. A ([[knight's fee|Knights]]) [[fee]], a [[manor|Mannor]], or inheritance held by [[homage]], and [[fealty]]; and given at the [[first|firſt]], in [[trust|truſt]], and upon [[promise|promiſe]] of [[assistance|aſſiſtance]], or [[service|ſervice]] in the wars: {{...}} [[also|Alſo]], a [[tenure|Tenure]], or [[estate|Eſtate]] [[in fief|in '''fief''']], or [[in fee]]. This word was [[first|firſt]] heard of, after the [[conquest|conqueſt]] of ''[[Gallia]]'' by the ''[[Franks|Francs]]'' (or ancient French-men) when their Soveraign Princes, [[reserving|reſerving]] [[some|ſome]] land for their own Domains, [[distributed|diſtributed]] the [[rest|reſt]] (by whole Countreys, or large territories) among their Captains, and principal followers, on condition, that they [[should|ſhould]] hold of them, and aid them in their wars {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Charles Poulett Thomson, 1st Baron Sydenham|C[harles] Poulett Thomson]]|chapter=An Ordinance to incorporate the Ecclesiastics of the Seminary of Saint Sulpice of Montreal [No. 164 of 1840]|date=8 June 1840|title=Copy of Ordinances Passed by the Governor and Special Council of Lower Canada, in the Third and Fourth Years of the Reign of Her Majesty Queen Victoria|series=Accounts and Papers. [[w:Parliament of the United Kingdom|United Kingdom. Parliament.]] [[w:House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]]|seriesvolume=1841, session 1|year_published=3 February 1841|volume=XV|pages=151–152|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_F4SAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA151|oclc=926570376|passage=And be it further ordained and enacted by the Authority aforesaid, That the Right and Title of the said Ecclesiastics of the Seminary of Saint Sulpice of Montreal, in and to all and singular the said '''Fiefs''' and Seigniories of the Island of Montreal, of the Lake of the Two Mountains, and of Saint Sulpice, and their several Dependencies, and in and to all Seigniorial and Feudal Rights, Privileges, Dues, and Duties arising out of and from the same, and in and to all and every the Domains, Lands, Reservations, Buildings, Messuages, Tenements, and Hereditaments within the said several '''Fiefs''' and Seigniories now held and possessed by them as Proprietors thereof, {{...}} shall be and they are hereby confirmed and declared good, valid, and effectual in the Law; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Franz Babinger|Franz [Carl Heinrich] Babinger]]|tlr=w:Ralph Manheim|editor=William C. Hickman|title=Mehmed the Conqueror and His Time|series=Bollingen Series|seriesvolume=XCVI|location=Princeton, N.J.|publisher=w:Princeton University Press|year=1992|page=446|isbn=978-0-691-09900-2|passage=The chief obligation of a ''{{w|sipahi}}'' was to take up residence on his '''fief''' and to be prepared at all times to rally, armed for battle, to his banner-holder's flag on the sultan's order. According to the income of his '''fief''', every ''sipahi'' had to raise a fixed number of armed horsemen (''cebeli''), who followed him on campaigns.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gerard J. Brault|chapter=The Death of Roland—Laisses 174–176 (verses 2355–2396)|title={{w|The Song of Roland}}: An Analytical Edition. Volume I. Introduction and Commentary|location=University Park, Pa.; London|publisher=[[w:Penn State University Press|The Pennsylvania State University Press]]|year=2010|page=256|isbn=978-0-271-00516-4|passage=Investiture was the conferring of a '''fief''' by the lord to the vassal, and the rite consisted in the handing over by the lord of some symbolic object intended to represent the act of concession.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} {{synonym of|en|estate}}: [[any]] [[land]], when [[consider]]ed as a [[region]] over which the [[owner]] [[exercise]]s [[lordly]] [[control]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Liliane Welch|chapter=Grandparents: A Fragment|editor=C. Dino Minni|title=Ricordi, Things Remembered: An Anthology of Short Stories|location=Montreal|publisher=w:Guernica Editions|year=1989|page=59|isbn=978-0-919349-97-1|passage=Through the years of my childhood my maternal grandmother remained the one unforgettable presence, the strong country woman ruling over her farm like a medieval lord. On her '''fief''' I first opened my eyes to poetry and to the land. {{...}} There was something of the ancient matriarch in her, who had given her life to the ground, who felt that on her '''fief''' in southern Luxembourg she stood in the right place.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[territory]], a [[domain]], an [[area]] over which [[one]] [[exercise]]s [[lordly]] [[control]], [[particularly]] [[with regard to]] [[corporate#Adjective|corporate]] or [[governmental]] [[bureaucracies]].

